		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=32252#p32252"><![CDATA[
I too wonder about this - and looking at some other physics systems most of them solve it with a 'transform' shape or some kind of offset. <br><br>One exception seems to be in Havok Physics, which has a center of mass (COM) vector which is applied when gathering the matrix for a body prior to solving. In my head I wonder is it as simple as that? When using the rigid body's matrix for solving, can we not just offset it by the COM vector, and then restore it after solving? <br><br>I plan on trying this as the overhead of another shape in the collision detection methods and the game-side shape hierarchy traversal shows up in our profiler (slow platform) and I would like to streamline the shape hierarchy and collision detection system. I am working with an existing proprietary physics engine that needs a lot of optimizations.<br><br>Any thoughts on this from other people - my maths are not super and so I likely am oversimplifying the problem.<br><br>S<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=10211">sphet</a> — Tue Nov 26, 2013 11:02 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
